\pard\sa200\sl240\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 This Is How I Humanize My Writing, as a Pro-AI Writer and Editor\par
1 principle, 1 framework, 0% AI \f1\emdash  99% of the time!\par
James Presbitero Jr.\par
\f0\par
Published in Write A Catalyst\par
5 min read Oct 9, 2024\par
\par
There\rquote s a growing problem in the writing industry.\par
That is, writers are being accused of using AI \f1\emdash  even though they aren\rquote t.\par
Whether you are pro-AI or anti-AI, I don\rquote t care. I know where both sides are coming from.\par
\par
But I do understand the frustration of writers whose works light up the AI detector platform \emdash  even if you toiled for it yourself. And it\rquote s deeper than the ego hit that you get when you\rquote re being accused.\par
How AI Accusations Hurt Writers\par
Sometimes this can actively hinder your opportunities to succeed.\par
Editors and content managers might reject your piece even if you didn\rquote t actually use AI to create them. Aside from just plain sucking, mentally and emotionally, this actively affects your income.\par
Of course, this situation is \emdash  as experts call it \emdash  f*ckin unfair.\par
I encounter this almost everyday, as an editor. It takes a lot of reassurance for my writers not to feel like their effort is being disregarded.\par
Unfortunately, this will likely be the industry norm moving forward \emdash  at least, until everyone gets informed about AI.\par
But it doesn\rquote t end there \emdash  there\rquote s another bitter pill to swallow.\par
While AI checkers are not entirely accurate, they do have data to prove they\rquote re on to something.\par
That\rquote s why I still use AI checkers for my work, even if they are not 100% accurate. I just don\rquote t base all of my edits on them.\par
What does this mean for you? If AI detectors flag your writing, that just means you have very general writing patterns.\par
This isn\rquote t necessarily bad. If you\rquote re writing for yourself, then who cares? Write how you want.\par
But if you write for a living, or are building a brand, there\rquote s merit in learning how to overcome AI detectors.\par
\par
The Superhuman Writing Principle\par
\ldblquote Passing\rdblquote  AI checkers means you have a distinct writing voice. That is beneficial, whatever purpose you\rquote re writing for.\par
In short, learning how to humanize content will help you stand out.\par
There\rquote s another benefit.\par
Once you know how to humanize your content, you can actually use AI effectively. All without being accused by AI checkers, or running afoul with your editors.\par
This level of writing, where you can safely leverage AI to write better, faster, and more human, is what I call Superhuman Writing.\par
I\rquote ve honed my skills while aiming for that level. I\rquote ve refined a framework that I use everyday to assist in my writing \emdash  without eclipsing my humanity, but rather enhancing it.\par
\par
\par
\par
I never get flagged by AI detectors.\par
In my work, I also help my writers have this mindset, and attain the same level of writing ability.\par
And it\rquote s all by using a simple framework.\par
\par
How I Humanize My Content: The Hourglass Framework\par
When editing AI content, I generally edit in two phases:\par
\par
Paring down\par
Building up\par
The paring-down shows you getting to the simplest iteration of what you are saying.\par
\par
This could be a fact, an insight, a good joke. Even if you\rquote re not using AI, this is good practice. Conciseness is key if you want your writing to shine. It was Blaise Pascal that said,\par
\ldblquote If I had more time, I would have written a shorter letter.\rdblquote\par
But this is most useful when using AI, because you end up with a whole lot of words \emdash  many of them have no business being there.\par
So, pare it down. Remove everything that doesn\rquote t serve a purpose. For this framework, each level represents some form of fluff that you should cut off:\par
Top Level: Hallucinations, circular statements, ineffective sentences or paragraphs.\par
Mid Level: Edit out weak phrases, weasel words.\par
Bottom Level: Fine-tune for conciseness. Replace unnecessarily complicated words (likely words with 3 syllables or more).\par
If you did this properly, you would end up with a piece that gets to the point. But this is not enough, of course.\par
Next is building up.\par
\par
In the image below, this represents the Tower. This is what most people actually mean when they\rquote re adding the \ldblquote human touch.\rdblquote  These are unpromtable elements \emdash  the things that make your piece truly yours.\par
I believe these are elements you can categorize to multiple levels.\par
Top Level: Style, voice, quirks \emdash  the stuff that aren\rquote t grammatically correct, but makes sense to our human minds.\par
Mid Level: Personal opinions, perspectives, strong stances, colorful stories.\par
Bottom Level: Personal, intimate vulnerabilities.\par
Here\rquote s a helpful infographic to make the points easier to remember.\par
